        "content": "Wirbeltiere",
        "evidence_anchor": "Forest habitat quality and extent, however, have been consistently decreasing globally (Millennium Ecosystem Assessment, 2005). Anthropogenic pressures and environmental changes are expected to further intensify forest degradation and threaten to jeopardize ecosystem functioning. Thus, conservation strategies are urgently needed in order to safeguard the provision of ecosystem services. Particularly, increasing diversity can lead to higher productivity and stability against climate extremes, promote resistance against invasive species and exert a positive impact on erosion control and nutrient cycling (Isbell et al., 2015). Therefore, biodiversity conservation needs to be included in forest management plans as an important goal, since it may act as an insurance to ecosystem functioning under changing environmental conditions (Baumgärtner, 2007)."
      },
      "Untergruppe_RoteListen": {
        "content": "Voegel",
        "evidence_anchor": "We employed data from the choice experiment developed by Weller and Elsasser (2017) to parametrize the household CES utility function. The authors assessed the WTP in Germany for an increase in the indicator of the National Biodiversity Strategy, which is quantified based on the abundance of 10 forest bird species, in comparison with their abundances in the 1970′s (Dröschmeister and Sukopp, 2009)."
      },
      "Lebensraum": {
        "content": "Wald",
        "evidence_anchor": "Forest biodiversity underpins social welfare by preserving ecosystem multifunctionality and the provision of ecosystem goods and services."
